/**
 * First-person spectator, pawn side. Included by every AdminModDW pawn class.
 *
 * AdminModDW changes no gameplay -- this override exists only so a spectator can see
 * through another player's eyes.
 *
 * DW delta, and it is a large one. The base-game AdminMod carries ~250 lines here for two
 * problems that Deadliest Warrior does not have:
 *
 *  1. Rebuilding the stripped 1P mesh. In the base game AOCPawn.SetCharacterAppearance
 *     DetachComponent()s OwnerMesh on every pawn that was not locally controlled when its
 *     character assets loaded, so BecomeFirstPersonObserved has nothing to put a mesh on.
 *     Deadliest Warrior predates the customization system: OwnerMesh is a plain default
 *     subobject (AOCPawn.uc:11008), the only thing done to it is SetHidden (AOCPawn.uc:7219,
 *     with the DetachComponent commented out beside it), and BecomeFirstPersonObserved
 *     assigns the mesh itself from PawnCharacter (AOCPawn.uc:10639). Nothing to rebuild.
 *
 *  2. The camera-socket fallback. That override hooked AOCPawn's
 *     GetCameraSocketLocationAndRotation. Deadliest Warrior has no such function --
 *     AOCPlayerCamera.UpdateCamera (:82) calls GetSocketWorldLocationAndRotation on the
 *     mesh component directly, so there is no script hook to interpose on. The pre-flight
 *     check in AdminModDWPlayerController.AdminModDWFPObserveBlocker is what keeps a pawn
 *     with no usable 1P mesh out of first person in the first place.
 *
 * What is left is the feint fix below, which Deadliest Warrior needs exactly as the base
 * game does.
 */

/* ************************************************************************
 * FEINT / BLEND RESET WHILE FIRST-PERSON OBSERVED
 *
 * AOCPawn.ResetBlends() (AOCPawn.uc:5061) opens with:
 *
 *   if (bReplicated && WorldInfo.NetMode != NM_DedicatedServer
 *       && ((IsLocallyControlled() && !bIsBot) || bIsBeingFPObserved))
 *       return;
 *
 * The bIsBeingFPObserved half assumes a first-person-observed pawn is OUR pawn, so the
 * local reset (bReplicated = false) has already run and the replicated one is redundant.
 * That holds for your own pawn. It is false for a remote pawn you are spectating: nothing
 * on this client ever ran the local reset.
 *
 * A feint goes ManualReset() -> replicate SResetBlends -> remote clients receive it in
 * ReplicatedEvent and call ResetBlends(ResetTime) with bReplicated defaulted TRUE. For the
 * pawn we are first-person observing that call returns immediately, the blends are never
 * cleared, and the weapon stays stuck in its feinted pose on the spectator's screen.
 *
 * Run it as a local reset rather than skipping it.
 **************************************************************************/
simulated function ResetBlends(optional float BlendOutTime = 0.3f, optional bool bReplicated = true)
{
	if (bReplicated && bIsBeingFPObserved && !IsLocallyControlled())
	{
		// bReplicated = false clears vanilla's early-out and performs the real reset,
		// which also calls AOCWeaponAttachment.ResetAnimation() -- the bit that puts
		// the weapon back to idle.
		super.ResetBlends(BlendOutTime, false);
		return;
	}

	super.ResetBlends(BlendOutTime, bReplicated);
}
